{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC4 follow-up: the test module was not runnable on Windows, so the native proof was unreadable\n\nThe production leg merged as `9d5a7278c892` and the sweep shows it WORKING. What was broken was\nthis story's own test module, `tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py`: 7/12 red on\n`windows-latest` in run `33956014400` / job `101279390978`. Both native tests appeared in that log\nneither as a pass nor as a failure, so AC4 could only be argued from absence. Verified both defects\nagainst the log before touching anything.\n\n**Defect 1 — five ERRORs, all at `_windows_shaped_msvcrt()` (`:169`), `ModuleNotFoundError: No\nmodule named 'fcntl'`.** That helper is the POSIX-HOST SIMULATION: its stub's `locking` delegates to\nthe host's real `flock`, which is exactly what makes the layer-2 assertions observations rather than\na mock's say-so — and exactly what Windows does not have. It cannot be built on Windows and must not\nbe. The `windows_leg` fixture now skips on `os.name == \"nt\"` with that reason stated. This removes\nno Windows coverage: on Windows those same code paths run against the REAL `msvcrt` throughout the\ndefault suite, and the primitive's own guarantees are `test_native_*`'s job.\n\n**Defect 2 — two FAILUREs, `test_no_leg_acquire_refuses_loudly_and_never_takes_the_mkdir_leg`\n(\"acquire succeeded with NO exclusive leg held\") and `test_no_leg_doctor_probe_reports_unknown`\n(`free` instead of `unknown`).** The premise was false on Windows: the child hid `fcntl` alone, and\n`msvcrt` is still importable there, so the selector correctly found a leg. Note this is POSITIVE\nevidence for the production leg — the real `acquire()` took the real msvcrt leg on `windows-latest`.\nThe assertion was NOT weakened. The premise was made true on both platforms: the child now has NO\nleg of either kind. `fcntl` is still hidden the module way (`sys.modules[...] = None`, which keeps\nproving rebar imports without it); `msvcrt` cannot be hidden that way, because on Windows the\nstandard library itself imports it at module scope (`subprocess` does) — a `sys.modules` entry would\nbreak the interpreter, not rebar. It is blanked at the ONE handle rebar reads, `lock_kernel`'s\nmodule-level primitive, which is the same seam the layer-2 fixture already patches. On POSIX that\nhandle is already `None`, so the assignment is a no-op. The premise is now asserted out loud\n(`assert _kernel.leg_name() == \"none\"`), so a future platform with a third leg fails the child\nimmediately instead of quietly voiding the test — checked by mutation (re-supply a leg ⇒ the child\nexits 1 on that assertion).\n\n**Readability — the reason AC4 was unprovable even when green.** The sweep's default suite runs\nunder `pytest -q`, where a passing test prints nothing. `sweep (windows)` now runs this one module\nwith `-v` BEFORE the default suite (before, because a failing step stops the ones after it, and this\nis the evidence worth keeping), naming each verdict in the log. It gates nothing — the job is\n`continue-on-error` — and adds no coverage; it re-runs an already-running module so the proof is\nlegible. Justified to the mechanism-delta ratchet in place as `mechanism-ok: ci_gate`.\n\n**No production code touched.** POSIX behaviour byte-for-byte unchanged: the module is still\n10 passed / 2 skipped, the three AC5 concurrency suites are 39/39, `make lint` and `make typecheck`\nclean.\n\nOut of scope and NOT duplicated: `_store/fsutil.py:82`'s bare `import fcntl` already has a ticket —\n`hyperbolic-humanoid-owl` (`658e-a99b-a004-4be3`), open p2, a child of the epic that `depends_on`\nthis story.\n\nAC4 stays UNTICKED until the re-dispatched sweep names both `test_native_*` as PASSED."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788604698586116963,"uuid":"c7962cb1-1072-45ed-9443-c5a6d2a4a636"}